## The Four Process Activities: RetailSync Case Study
RetailSync did a lot of things wrong with this new system. 

- In stage one it is described that "the IT director held a single one-hour meeting". 
In this meeting "no written requirements document was produced". 
This is a terrible way to start out as the developement team has no idea what they are actually supposed to be building. 

- In stage two "The team of three developers worked largely independently, each taking a module (stock intake, stock transfer, reporting) with minimal coordination between them"
This lack of cummunication has lead to an incohesive team that are not complimenting eachother in any way. 
"There were no shared coding standards and no code review process — each developer pushed code directly to a shared folder on a network drive, occasionally overwriting a colleague's changes without noticing"
In this piece of quoted text it can be seen that the team were not performing any validation when pushing their code to the shared folder. 
This will lead to crashes almost certainly. 

- "Warehouse staff were not consulted during this period; the developers assumed their understanding of 'how a warehouse works' from the kickoff meeting was sufficient."
This quoted text again shows that the developers were not in communication with their clients leading to a product that does not fit the needs. 
This is again an example of poor specifications from the IT director. 

- During the developement the team where told that the system would "need to support the company's new second warehouse, which used a different shelving and barcode scheme."
This is comunicated with the team much too late and is causing them to have to do a massive evolution on the system. 
However the developement team did not evolve the system but instead they "did not revisit the existing design, instead patching the second warehouse's requirements on top of the existing schema."
this is a major red flag as there is no way of knowing that these systems will work together. 

- In the testing phase the team again has ignored all protocol and gone ahead with testing without consulting with the customer. 
They "fixed the crashes they personally encountered" without testing to see for the crashes they did not encounter. 
"No warehouse staff tested the system before go-live", not good. 

- It seems that throughout this developement of this piece of software the developement team completely ignored the Four Processes and went with their own version that had very pool results. 
There was little-to-no specification from the IT director and the team suddenly knew what they were doing. 
There was a very poor developement workflow and the team were not working together in any of the stages. 
Validation was not considered at all as the team were pushing their code and deleting others code to make their own work all the time. 
Lastly they did not evolve the system when they were told that the system had to encorperate the older stocking system. 

In my opinion the single greatest failure that did the most damage is the lack of specifications and communication from the IT director. 
This lead to the team making their own assumptions about what was required of the software when in actuallity they hadn't once consulted with anyone working on the warehouse team. 




## Researching a Softeare Failure 
Therac-25
The Therac-25 was a radiation therapy machine that was used to treat cancer patients. 
Between 1985 and 1987, there were several accidents where patients where given massive amounts of radiation, which caused serious injuries and deaths. 
The main problem was that the machine relied heavily on its software to make sure it was working safely. 
There was a software error which meant that if the machine was used in a certain way, it could give a patient a much higher amount of radiation than it was supposed to.
The developers also failed to find the problem early enough becuase they believed that the problem was caused by the hardware and not the software. 

I think this failure links to the complexity of software enfineering.
The Therac-25 had many different parts that all had to work together correctly. 
This shows one small software error can cause a much bigger problem when it is part of a complex system. 
